        "created_at": "2013-09-19T22:27:36.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I see your point with 0,1 and null. But we are evaluating the rules based inside a defined system. These rules and cards do not exist in a vacuum.&nbsp;</p><p>There is no effect without a cause. Learned that in science class. When no cause to an effect is given it is a much safer assumption to say the cause/trigger has already occurred rather than create your own trigger from thin air.</p><p>The Trigger/Effect structure of the game is the principle system allowing the game to function. It was a very conscious decision by &gt;G to design the system that way as it allows new players to dive right in without needing a huge rulebook. Magic the Gathering, by and large, is 80% an implicit rules system. SotM is the exact opposite, opting to not use effect keywords and such so that almost every effect and interaction is explicit and written on the card. The absence of an explicit trigger should be a strong indication that the trigger is tied to a core implicit part of the rules instead of triggering off something that happens in game (for instance, whenever a hero deals damage seems to be a popular trigger people have invented for applying damage mods). I understand that without official word it's all assumptions here. But there is no strong reason to accept an alternate interpretation as correct at this point.&nbsp;</p><p>This really has more to do with damage modification in general and not a lot to do with Unity. I don't think anyone doubts how she and Legacy work together.&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
